%% SECTION Risk
@@
Which element of the risk management plan defines the scales used to rate probability and impact?
A) Definitions of risk probability and impact
B) Risk register entries for each identified risk
C) Risk breakdown structure categories
D) Stakeholder risk appetite statements
= A
? The risk management plan contains probability and impact definitions and the probability-impact matrix used in analysis.

@@
Which statement is written in the recommended cause–risk–effect format?
A) Because the vendor is new to us, delivery of components may be late, which would delay integration testing by two weeks
B) The vendor is new to us and has not yet delivered any components, so integration testing is a major concern for the team
C) Integration testing may be delayed by two weeks, which is a serious risk that the vendor needs to manage carefully
D) Late delivery of components by the vendor is an issue that will cause problems in testing and must be escalated
= A
? A good risk statement links a cause, an uncertain event and its effect on objectives.

@@
A risk is rated with high probability and high impact but its effect will not be felt for eight months. Which risk parameter captures how soon the risk could affect objectives?
A) Proximity
B) Dormancy
C) Manageability
D) Detectability
= A
? Proximity is the time before the risk could affect objectives. Dormancy is the time between occurrence and discovery of its effect.

@@
Which technique helps reduce bias by gathering expert opinions anonymously over several rounds until they converge?
A) Delphi technique
B) Brainstorming
C) Nominal group technique
D) SWOT analysis
= A
? The Delphi technique uses anonymous, iterative rounds with a facilitator to reach consensus without dominance by individuals.

@@
Which output summarises the sources of overall project risk and the status of individual risks for stakeholders?
A) Risk report
B) Risk register
C) Risk breakdown structure
D) Assumption log
= A
? The risk report presents overall project risk and summary information on individual risks; the register lists individual risks in detail.

@@
To reduce the probability that a new technology will fail in production, the team builds a prototype and runs a pilot. Which threat response is this?
A) Mitigate
B) Avoid
C) Transfer
D) Accept
= A
? Mitigation reduces probability and/or impact. Avoiding would remove the threat entirely, for example by not using the technology.

@@
The team decides to use a proven technology instead of a new one to eliminate a technical threat. Which response is this?
A) Avoid
B) Mitigate
C) Exploit
D) Escalate
= A
? Changing the plan to eliminate the threat is avoidance.

@@
Two companies form a joint venture to win a contract that neither could win alone. Which opportunity response is this?
A) Share
B) Exploit
C) Enhance
D) Transfer
= A
? Sharing allocates ownership of an opportunity to a party better able to capture it, such as a partner.

@@
The project sets aside two weeks of schedule reserve to cope with possible weather delays and takes no other action. Which strategy is this?
A) Active acceptance
B) Passive acceptance
C) Mitigation
D) Avoidance
= A
? Active acceptance establishes a contingency reserve. Passive acceptance takes no action except periodic review.

@@
After a vendor contract with penalty clauses was signed to transfer schedule risk, the vendor raised its prices for the next phase. How is this new risk of higher costs classified?
A) Secondary risk
B) Residual risk
C) Trigger
D) Workaround
= A
? A secondary risk arises directly from implementing a risk response.

@@
Which quantitative technique runs thousands of iterations using probability distributions to estimate the likelihood of meeting a target date?
A) Monte Carlo simulation
B) Probability and impact matrix
C) SWOT analysis
D) Checklist analysis
= A
? Monte Carlo simulation models combined uncertainty to produce probability distributions of outcomes.

@@
A decision tree shows Option A with an expected value of +$40,000 and Option B with an expected value of +$55,000. Both are opportunities. Which option should be chosen based on EMV?
A) Option B
B) Option A
C) Neither, because decision trees are qualitative
D) Both, combined
= A
? For opportunities, choose the higher expected value; for costs, choose the lower expected cost.

@@
Who is responsible for implementing the agreed response when a risk's trigger occurs?
A) The risk owner
B) The sponsor
C) The customer
D) The procurement department
= A
? Each risk has a risk owner responsible for monitoring it and implementing the response.

@@
During a risk review, the team finds several risks that are no longer relevant. What should happen?
A) Close them and consider releasing the related contingency reserve as the plan allows
B) Keep them open for the rest of the project in case the conditions change again
C) Delete them from the register so that the document stays short and readable
D) Transfer them to the customer, who is better placed to watch for the conditions that trigger them
= A
? Risk reassessment closes outdated risks; reserve analysis may release unneeded reserves.

@@ | multi
Which two techniques are typically used in qualitative risk analysis? (Choose two.)
A) Probability and impact matrix
B) Assessment of risk urgency and proximity
C) Monte Carlo simulation
D) Decision tree with EMV
E) Tornado diagram from sensitivity analysis
= A, B
? Qualitative analysis prioritises risks using probability, impact and other parameters. Simulation, EMV decision trees and sensitivity analysis are quantitative.

@@
A project manager identifies a risk that a sister program might reprioritise shared resources. The project cannot influence this decision. What is the best response?
A) Escalate it to the program or portfolio manager who owns the decision
B) Accept it passively, since the project cannot influence the decision
C) Mitigate it by hiring new staff now in case the resources are withdrawn
D) Transfer it by buying insurance against the loss of shared resources
= A
? The risk lies outside the project's authority, so it should be escalated to the appropriate owner.

@@
In an adaptive project, how are high-risk backlog items usually handled?
A) They are often prioritised early to reduce uncertainty quickly
B) They are postponed to the final sprint when the team is most experienced
C) They are removed from the backlog until the risk has disappeared
D) They are handed to the sponsor, who owns all product risks
= A
? Tackling risky items early validates assumptions and reduces risk exposure; risk responses can be added to the backlog.

@@
Which document lists assumptions whose possible inaccuracy should be examined as a source of risk?
A) Assumption log
B) Stakeholder register
C) Change log
D) Team charter
= A
? Assumption and constraint analysis examines the assumption log for assumptions that might be inaccurate, unstable or incomplete.

@@
What is the main difference between a contingency plan and a fallback plan?
A) A contingency plan runs when a trigger occurs; a fallback plan is used if it fails
B) A fallback plan runs when a trigger occurs; a contingency plan is used if it fails
C) Both terms describe the same plan, prepared for risks above the threshold
D) A contingency plan handles issues, while a fallback plan handles opportunities
= A
? Fallback plans are backups for when the primary contingent response is insufficient.

@@
A project has an aggressive risk appetite, while its client is very risk-averse. What should the project manager do?
A) Surface the differences with key stakeholders and agree thresholds in the risk management plan
B) Follow the project team's appetite, since the team is responsible for delivering all of the work
C) Ignore risk appetite, since it is subjective and cannot be measured or documented
D) Remove the client from risk discussions so that the team can work without pressure
= A
? Stakeholder risk appetites shape thresholds; differences should be surfaced and agreed early.
